<!-- Codec2 components provided by external/v4l2_codec2, for the DomA
     hardware-decode path on this board.

     DECODERS ONLY: HEVC in hardware, and H.264 in software in DomD. The
     RPi4 file has one H.264 entry because VideoCore decodes H.264 there;
     this SoC has no H.264 hardware.

     BCM2712 has no VCHIQ node at all. Measured by decompiling the shipped DTBs:
     zero occurrences in bcm2712-rpi-5-b.dtb against two in
     bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dtb, where brcm,bcm2711-vchiq sits at
     /soc/mailbox@7e00b840. bcm2835-codec reaches VideoCore's H.264 decoder only
     over VCHIQ and has no DT node of its own, so on this SoC it has nothing to
     bind to: H.264 hardware decode is not unported here, it is absent.

     What this SoC does have is codec@800000, brcm,bcm2712-hevc-dec, driven by
     rpi-hevc-dec: the hevc entry.

     THE avc ENTRY IS NOT HARDWARE. It is the device model's libavcodec
     software decoder in DomD (qemu q11-072 to q11-074), there because DomD
     plays H.264 1080p60 at 60 fps where DomA's own software decoder,
     c2.android.avc on two vCPUs, reached 35.4 (board, 2026-10-15). v4l2_codec2
     lists c2.v4l2.avc.decoder only when the device offers H.264
     (decoder=hevc+h264, RPI5_DOMA_H264 in meta-xt-rpi5) and then picks it
     before c2.android.avc; without that, Codec2InfoBuilder lists only the
     components the store has, and this entry is not used.

     WHY THERE IS AN ENTRY AT ALL, GIVEN THE DRIVER IS STATELESS. rpi-hevc-dec
     wants a media device and a request fd per frame, which virtio-media cannot
     carry, and Android's v4l2_codec2 has no stateless support. qemu patch 0217
     therefore presents a STATEFUL HEVC decoder to the guest and drives the
     stateless side with libavcodec in DomD. From this file's point of view the
     device is an ordinary stateful decoder, which is why the entry looks
     unremarkable.

     No VP8/VP9/AV1: the template in external/v4l2_codec2/README.md lists them
     and none has hardware here.

     No encoders: the device model's decoder mode decodes only.

     No .secure variant: it needs Widevine and a secure allocator, neither of
     which exists on this product.

     HEVC up to 3840x2176: a stream wider than the display reaches DomA at
     the display's width, scaled down by DomD's GPU conversion
     (host-max-width, RPI5_DOMA_HWDEC_MAX_W in meta-xt-rpi5), and 4K60 plays
     at 56 to 61 fps (board, 2026-10-16). The frame's MediaFormat
     width and height stay the stream's; the crop is the scaled size, which
     a Surface shows full size. One 4K stream at a time: a second one ran
     DomD's CMA out (384 MiB then) and failed in its client (board,
     2026-10-16), although concurrent-instances is 8; the 640 MiB CMA keeps
     a 4K stream's frames referenced for the display, and two were not
     tried. H.264 stays at 1920x1088, as in the RPi4 file: H.264 above
     1080p reaches DomA scaled, but the software decoder plays 2560x1440
     60p at 39.6 fps (board, 2026-10-16).

     HOW THE NUMBERS BELOW WERE DERIVED.

     blocks-per-second is the ARITHMETIC MAXIMUM, as in the RPi4 file:
     1920x1088 in 16x16 blocks is 120 x 68 = 8160, times 60 = 489600 (avc);
     3840x2176 is 240 x 136 = 32640, times 60 = 1958400 (hevc).

     performance-point-1920x1080 is 30. Measured on the board (2026-09-24)
     with AOSP's codec tool on a 1080p stream, the decode alone ran at 57 fps
     and at 45 fps with the display, so 30 describes the decoder; playback in
     an app (VideoView) showed 15-19 frames a second on the same day.

     NEITHER NUMBER REACHES WEBVIEW (measured 2026-08-30 on the RPi4, which
     ships the same WebView). The shipped WebView 145.0.7632.218 references
     getVideoCapabilities, getSupportedWidths, isSizeSupported and
     isFeatureSupported in its dex, but NOT areSizeAndRateSupported and NOT
     getSupportedPerformancePoints, and neither appears in
     libwebviewchromium.so. Chromium selects on size and features alone, so
     blocks-per-second and the performance point cannot cap what a page
     playing video in a WebView asks for. performance-point is a pure
     information API in the framework too: every reference to
     getSupportedPerformancePoints() is a getter, a JNI read-out or the
     interface declaration, never codec selection.

     The avc entry has no performance point and the bitrate of H.264 level
     4.2 High (62.5 Mbit/s). concurrent-instances is 2, the most that was
     measured, and two already fall short: 720p60 and 1080p60 together
     played at 58.0 and 50.4 fps (2026-10-16), DomD's CPU being the limit.

     NOTE for whoever edits this file: an XML comment may not contain two
     consecutive hyphens. It is worse than a build break: MediaCodecsXmlParser
     reports BAD_VALUE,
     combineStatus() does NOT downgrade that to OK, and Codec2InfoBuilder.cpp:503
     then returns without registering ANY Codec2 component. A stray double hyphen
     makes the hardware decoder vanish from MediaCodecList entirely.
-->
<MediaCodecs>
   <Decoders>
       <MediaCodec name="c2.v4l2.hevc.decoder" type="video/hevc" >
           <Limit name="size" min="64x64" max="3840x2176" />
           <Limit name="alignment" value="2x2" />
           <Limit name="block-size" value="16x16" />
           <Limit name="blocks-per-second" min="1" max="1958400" />
           <Limit name="bitrate" range="1-120000000" />
           <Limit name="concurrent-instances" max="8" />
           <Limit name="performance-point-1920x1080" range="30-30" />
           <Feature name="adaptive-playback" />
       </MediaCodec>
       <MediaCodec name="c2.v4l2.avc.decoder" type="video/avc" >
           <Limit name="size" min="64x64" max="1920x1088" />
           <Limit name="alignment" value="2x2" />
           <Limit name="block-size" value="16x16" />
           <Limit name="blocks-per-second" min="1" max="489600" />
           <Limit name="bitrate" range="1-62500000" />
           <Limit name="concurrent-instances" max="2" />
           <Feature name="adaptive-playback" />
       </MediaCodec>
   </Decoders>
</MediaCodecs>
